      "avalancheActivity": {
        "comment": "Weak layers in the lower part of the snowpack can be released even by individual winter sport participants. This applies on steep west, north and east facing slopes above approximately 2600 m, as well as in all aspects above approximately 3000 m. Remotely triggered avalanches are possible in isolated cases. This applies in high Alpine regions. Mostly avalanches are medium-sized. <br/> In addition the fresh wind slabs should be taken into account. Such avalanche prone locations are to be found in steep terrain above approximately 2400 m. Caution is to be exercised adjacent to ridgelines, as well as in gullies and bowls, and behind abrupt changes in the terrain. The number and size of avalanche prone locations will increase with altitude. Avalanches can reach medium size. <br/> The Avalanche Warning Service currently has only a small amount of information that has been collected in the field, so that the avalanche danger should be investigated especially thoroughly in the relevant locality.",
        "highlights": "Weak layers in the old snowpack necessitate caution. Fresh wind slabs require caution."
      },
